  "name": "Example: Determining Whether Points on Intersecting Lines Are Collinear",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/2f6d703c604c780da8c97a012b94e75f06a55aa8b6e706a1097dc33ff193e88c.png)\n\nGiven the picture shown above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The points $K$, $N$, and $Z$ are collinear\n\nII. The points $K$, $P$, and $J$ are collinear",
  "worked_solution": "- Statement I is false. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/390f01ec8631f4c4eca0f391dd5b1c2f9e450e94d3bd992e6046c42dff0fc1e8.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/d9d250f402d238388931ec077d17afe65b248d245ae6333f9cb150c18288ccce.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
